{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790334709,"nonce":"009pV75WFT7z2G+tctayGOfDfhM=","title":"Runtime `macro class` built nothing; nested array patterns never tested; typedef-of-Array receivers lost Array's methods","message":"`macro class X { ... }` outside a macro kept only a placeholder, so Printer.printTypeDefinition printed garbage (Issue3205, 8859, 9565, after e4eefbb9 let Printer compile). Chasing that exposed:\n- `case C([]):`, an array pattern nested in a constructor pattern, became a wildcard, so it matched any array.\n- A TypePath with `sub` (`haxe.macro.Expr` sub `TypeDefinition`, as macro-built TPaths carry it) resolved to the module type and ignored the sub-type.\n- `a.map(f)` on a typedef of Array or a `Null\u003cArray\u003e` returned an Array typed by the receiver's element, so join read the mapped strings as ints or boxes.\n- A field typed with a typedef of Array whose use was typed before its declaration (`TypeParamDecl.meta : Metadata`) was registered as a class, so `.map`, `[i]` and friends took class paths.\n\nFix: the parser keeps `macro class/interface/typedef` as ExprKind::TypeDecl, and runtime reification builds the TypeDefinition (fields, access, FVar/FProp/FFun, type params with meta and defaults, ComplexTypes). Array params in constructor patterns take the guard desugar. `sub` is normalised to `pack.Module.Sub`. map's result looks through Null and typedefs. Field reads and method receivers typed as such a class read as the Array.\n\nConformance 666 -\u003e 668 (Issue3205, Issue9565), 0 LOST. Issue8859's second assertion expects a Printer newer than upstream 4.3.6 (identical to ours), which prints `var` for every FProp: not reachable with upstream-shaped std.","files":null}]}